Chapter 2
Volatility, time, and capital adequacy
التقلب والزمن وكفاية رأس المال
Chapter 1 named the families of risk. This chapter supplies the two quantities every one of them is measured against — how much a thing moves, and how long it is exposed — and then asks the question those two quantities exist to answer: is there enough capital behind the business to survive what the movement might do?
Volatility and time are the raw material of every risk measure in this book. Expected loss, value at risk, earnings at risk, the insurance premium, the covenant headroom: each is a statement about how far something might move within some period. And capital adequacy is the answer to the only question that ultimately matters, which is not how much might be lost but whether the company would still be there afterwards.
Figure F802-01 is your map.
Figure F802-01. Chapter 2 roadmap: how much it moves, how long it is exposed, and whether there is enough behind it.
