2.2  How time impacts risk
Time is the second thing that makes a risk large, and it works in three distinct ways that the outcome expects to be distinguished.
The longer the horizon, the wider the distribution. A quantity that moves randomly drifts further from its starting point the longer it is allowed to move. Where the movements in successive periods are independent, the standard deviation grows with the square root of time: four years of exposure carries twice the one-year dispersion, not four times.
The longer the horizon, the more likely a rare event becomes. An event with a 5 per cent chance in any one year has a 1 − 0.5987 = 0.4013 chance of happening at least once in ten, because 0.95 multiplied by itself ten times is 0.5987. Nothing about the event has changed; only the window has.
And the longer the horizon, the less anybody knows. Beyond a few years the estimates themselves become unreliable, which is the uncertainty of Chapter 1 rather than risk, and it is why Book 3 put a terminal value on everything after year five rather than pretending to forecast year eleven.
Two consequences run through the rest of this book.
Risk measures must state their horizon. "A value at risk of 400,000" means nothing until somebody says over what period, and a daily figure and an annual figure differ by a factor of about sixteen.
And exposure can be managed by shortening time as well as by reducing movement. A shorter payment term reduces credit exposure without changing any customer's creditworthiness; a hedge that matures in three months leaves the fourth month exposed; holding stock for sixty days rather than ninety reduces the price risk in it by a third of the period. Chapter 5's responses include several that work only on the clock.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: scaling volatility with time
Step 1. The one-year figure. Orontes's operating income has a standard deviation of 731,900 over a year.
Step 2. Four years. Where the years are independent, the four-year dispersion is the one-year figure times the square root of four, which is 731,900 × 2 = 1,463,800.
Step 3. A quarter. Going the other way, the quarterly figure is the annual one divided by the square root of four, which is 731,900 ÷ 2 = 365,950.
Step 4. The assumption. All of this holds only where the periods are independent. Orontes's sales are seasonal and its sesame price is persistent, so the real four-year dispersion is wider than the rule suggests in some respects and narrower in others.
Check. The square-root rule is a convention, not a law, and the examiners test whether you know what it assumes. Where movements are positively correlated from period to period — a trend, a persistent price, a reputation that decays slowly — the rule understates the long-horizon risk. That is the single most important caveat in this chapter.
