2.3  Capital adequacy: solvency
Capital adequacy is the condition of having enough capital, of the right kinds and in the right places, to absorb the losses a business might suffer and still continue. The term comes from banking and insurance, where regulators impose it, but the concept applies to every company and the outcome asks for its four elements: solvency, liquidity, reserves and sufficient capital.
Solvency is the first: that assets exceed liabilities, so that the company could meet its obligations if everything were realised. It is a balance-sheet condition and it is measured by the equity cushion.
Orontes's equity is $20,392,975 against total assets of $31,672,600, so equity funds 20,392,975 ÷ 31,672,600 = 64.39 per cent of the assets. Read as a risk statement, that says asset values could fall by nearly two thirds before the creditors were threatened — which is a different and more useful reading of the same ratio Book 1 computed as a measure of gearing.
Three cautions belong with the solvency test and all three appear in questions.
It depends on the measurement of the assets. Book 1's Chapter 7 showed goodwill being impaired by $2,724,000, and an impairment is the discovery that the solvency cushion was smaller than the balance sheet said.
It is a point in time. A company solvent at the year end can be insolvent in March, and the test that matters is solvency after the loss, not before it.
And solvency is not survival. A company can be solvent and still fail, which is the subject of the next section and the most examined distinction in this chapter.
