2.4  Capital adequacy: liquidity
Liquidity is the ability to meet obligations as they fall due, in cash, at the moment they fall due. It is a flow condition rather than a balance-sheet one, and it is the element that actually kills companies: the great majority of corporate failures are liquidity failures in businesses that were solvent on paper.
The distinction is worth stating in one line, because the exam tests it directly. Solvency asks whether there is enough. Liquidity asks whether it is available in time and in the right form.
Orontes's position is strong. It holds $7,302,100 of cash and $4,144,000 of receivables, which together are 7,302,100 + 4,144,000 = 11,446,100 of near-cash against payables of $2,050,000 — a cover of 11,446,100 ÷ 2,050,000 = 5.58 times. Book 1 read that as a sign of cash sitting idle; read as a risk statement it is a buffer, and the tension between those two readings is a real one that Chapter 4 resolves with the cost-benefit test.
Four sources of liquidity exist and a company should know the size and reliability of each.
Cash and deposits, which are certain and earn little.
Undrawn committed facilities, which are nearly certain — subject to the covenants of Book 2's Chapter 5, which are exactly the conditions most likely to be breached in the circumstances where the facility is needed.
Assets that can be sold or financed, which are reliable in calm markets and least reliable in the moment they are called on, because everybody else is selling the same things.
And the operating cycle itself: receivables that will be collected and stock that will be sold, whose timing is the subject of Book 1's Chapter 12.
That ordering is the lesson. Liquidity sources behave worst at exactly the moment they are needed, which is why a liquidity buffer is held in cash and committed facilities and not in the hope of selling something.
