LANGUAGE FOCUS   "Reserve", "provision" and "capital"
Few words cause more confusion in English than reserve. In accounting a reserve is part of equity and describes where money came from; in insurance and banking a reserve is an amount set aside against a loss; in ordinary speech a reserve is something held back for later, which is closer to the second. Provision is the narrower accounting term for a liability of uncertain timing or amount, and in English practice provision is the correct word where a loss is expected, with reserve kept for equity. Arabic احتياطي covers both senses and مخصص is the provision, which is clearer than the English. French distinguishes réserve, part of capitaux propres, from provision, a charge against profit, with the same strictness. And capital in this chapter means the loss-absorbing equity of a business, not the capital of Book 3's capital budgeting, which means money invested in assets. Where a question uses the word, read which of the two it means before answering.
