Chapter summary
Figure F802-03 brings the chapter together.
Figure F802-03. Chapter 2 summary map: the key rule for each section.
Volatility is dispersion about an average, measured by the standard deviation and, relatively, by the coefficient of variation — Orontes's operating income averaging 20,376,000 ÷ 5 = 4,075,200 with a standard deviation of 731,900, a coefficient of 731,900 ÷ 4,075,200 = 17.96 per cent — and it is measured from history, so it describes ordinary trading rather than the tail. Time widens the distribution with the square root of its length, so four years carry 731,900 × 2 = 1,463,800 of dispersion where the periods are independent; it makes rare events likely, a 5 per cent annual event arriving at least once in ten years with probability 1 − 0.5987 = 0.4013; and it degrades every estimate, which is why exposure can be managed by shortening the clock as well as by reducing the movement. Capital adequacy has four elements. Solvency is assets above liabilities, Orontes's equity funding 20,392,975 ÷ 31,672,600 = 64.39 per cent of its assets. Liquidity is funds available in time and in the right form, and it is what actually kills companies: 7,302,100 + 4,144,000 = 11,446,100 of near-cash covers payables 11,446,100 ÷ 2,050,000 = 5.58 times, and the reliability of liquidity sources falls in exactly the order they are needed. Reserves cover expected loss and capital absorbs unexpected loss, with the extreme tail managed by transfer, limits and continuity planning rather than by holding capital against everything.
