Case-style practice: the treasurer's proposal
Orontes's treasurer proposes to reduce the cash balance from $7,302,100 to $3,000,000, investing the difference in a three-year bond that cannot be sold before maturity without a penalty, and to replace the lost flexibility with an uncommitted overdraft facility from the company's bank.
Required. Evaluate the proposal in terms of solvency, liquidity and sufficient capital, using the figures from this chapter and Chapter 1. Say what you would want changed before agreeing. Under 350 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC2-1  Answer A. Dispersion, in either direction.
B is wrong. Direction is trend, not volatility.
C is wrong. That is a probability, not a dispersion.
D is wrong. Size is not movement.
SC2-2  Answer A. 731,900 ÷ 4,075,200 = 17.96 per cent.
B is wrong. This inverts the ratio.
C is wrong. That is the standard deviation itself.
D is wrong. That is the complement, which has no meaning here.
SC2-3  Answer A. It is relative, so size cancels.
B is wrong. The mean is its denominator.
C is wrong. It measures dispersion.
D is wrong. It is estimated from history like everything else.
SC2-4  Answer A. The square root of four is two.
B is wrong. That would be linear scaling.
C is wrong. That is four squared.
D is wrong. Time does change the figure.
SC2-5  Answer A. 1 − 0.5987 = 0.4013.
B is wrong. That is the one-year probability.
C is wrong. This has no basis in the arithmetic.
D is wrong. That is the one-year survival probability.
SC2-6  Answer A. Correlated movements compound rather than cancel.
B is wrong. Independence is the rule's assumption.
C is wrong. Negative correlation would overstate it.
D is wrong. Normality is a separate assumption.
SC2-7  Answer A. A balance-sheet condition.
B is wrong. That is liquidity.
C is wrong. Profits are a flow, not a cushion.
D is wrong. Market value is not the test.
SC2-8  Answer A. 20,392,975 ÷ 31,672,600 = 64.39 per cent.
B is wrong. That is the liabilities' share.
C is wrong. This inverts the ratio.
D is wrong. That misreads the figures.
SC2-9  Answer A. The cushion was overstated.
B is wrong. Impairment is non-cash.
C is wrong. No cash timing is involved.
D is wrong. No rule has been breached.
SC2-10  Answer A. Timing and form are the liquidity questions.
B is wrong. That is solvency.
C is wrong. Profit is neither.
D is wrong. Ownership is not the issue.
SC2-11  Answer A. 11,446,100 ÷ 2,050,000 = 5.58 times.
B is wrong. That uses cash alone.
C is wrong. That uses receivables alone.
D is wrong. This inverts the ratio.
SC2-12  Answer A. Markets are least liquid when everybody needs liquidity.
B is wrong. Cash is the most reliable.
C is wrong. A committed facility is contractual.
D is wrong. Money already received is certain.
SC2-13  Answer A. Reserves cover what is expected.
B is wrong. Capital covers the unexpected.
C is wrong. The extreme tail is transferred or limited.
D is wrong. They cover expected losses by definition.
SC2-14  Answer A. Capital absorbs the unexpected part.
B is wrong. That is reserved.
C is wrong. Operating costs are charged against revenue.
D is wrong. Interest is a claim, not a loss.
SC2-15  Answer A. Reserves are a label on equity, not cash.
B is wrong. Payment needs cash.
C is wrong. It may be entirely solvent.
D is wrong. Retained earnings are equity.
Your turn
@table
Item | Answer
Coefficient of variation | 17.96 per cent: 731,900 ÷ 4,075,200 = 17.96 per cent.
Four-year volatility | 1,463,800: 731,900 × 2 = 1,463,800, the square root of four being two.
Probability over ten years | About 40 per cent: 1 − 0.5987 = 0.4013.
Equity as a share of assets | 64.39 per cent: 20,392,975 ÷ 31,672,600 = 64.39 per cent.
Cash and receivables against payables | 5.58 times: 11,446,100 ÷ 2,050,000 = 5.58.
What covers each kind of loss | Reserves cover expected loss; capital absorbs unexpected loss.
What it tells you | The first five are measurements and the sixth is the principle they serve: measure the movement, measure the time, and then ask whether what stands behind the business is enough.
@end
