B is wrong. That is linear scaling.
C is wrong. That is nine squared.
D is wrong. That has no basis in the rule.
P2-05  Answer A. More windows, more chances.
B is wrong. The opposite is true.
C is wrong. The window matters.
D is wrong. Nothing becomes impossible with time.
P2-06  Answer A. Independence is the assumption.
B is wrong. Correlation breaks the rule.
C is wrong. So does negative correlation.
D is wrong. Identical movements are not random.
P2-07  Answer A. Assets above liabilities.
B is wrong. That is a liquidity test.
C is wrong. Profit is a flow.
D is wrong. Debt is compatible with solvency.
P2-08  Answer A. Solvent companies run out of cash.
B is wrong. The reverse is uncommon.
C is wrong. Unprofitable companies can survive on cash for years.
D is wrong. Compliance failures are rarely fatal on their own.
P2-09  Answer A. Asset sales fail when everybody is selling.
B is wrong. Cash is certain.
C is wrong. A committed facility is contractual.
D is wrong. Money received is already in hand.
P2-10  Answer A. A component of equity.
B is wrong. Reserves are not cash.
C is wrong. A provision is the liability.
D is wrong. Insurance is a contract.
P2-11  Answer A. Capital absorbs the unexpected.
B is wrong. Provisions cover the expected.
C is wrong. Revenue is not a buffer.
D is wrong. Receivables are an asset, not a cushion.
P2-12  Answer A. Depreciation policy does not change loss-absorbing capacity.
B is wrong. Both are central.
C is wrong. Appetite sets the tolerance.
D is wrong. External requirements bind directly.
Case-style set: the treasurer's proposal
Solvency is unaffected. Moving 4,302,100 from a deposit to a bond changes the form of an asset and not its amount, so equity still funds 64.39 per cent of the assets and the balance-sheet cushion is the same. If anything the bond improves reported returns, since idle cash earns least, and Book 1's criticism of a cash balance this size stands.
Liquidity is materially affected, in two ways the proposal understates. First, the near-cash cover falls: cash and receivables of 11,446,100 against payables of $2,050,000 becomes about 7,144,000, so the cover falls from 5.58 times to about 3.5. That is still comfortable for ordinary trading. Second, and more seriously, an uncommitted facility is not a liquidity source. The bank may withdraw it, and it will withdraw it in precisely the circumstances that would make Orontes need it. Section 2.4's ordering applies: cash and committed facilities are reliable; everything else is reliable only while it is not needed.
Sufficient capital is the test the proposal fails. Chapter 1's sixty-day stoppage costs 2,761,620 of contribution, and the bad-year combination of section 2.5 left the year 264,372 below zero before any rebuilding. The cash balance is the only thing that funds a rebuilding while the business generates nothing, and the proposal locks away most of it for three years with a penalty for early exit — exactly the wrong maturity against a risk whose timing is unknown.
What I would want changed. Three things, and I would then support it. Replace the uncommitted overdraft with a committed facility, documented, with covenant headroom tested against the bad-year scenario rather than against the budget. Reduce the amount invested, or ladder it so that part matures each year, so that the lock-up is not total. And set the retained cash balance from the scenario rather than from a round number: the figure that matters is the rebuilding cost plus a year of fixed claims, not $3,000,000 chosen because it is tidy.
